Cost of equity is shareholders' required return for bearing equity risk.|Assumed risk-free 5% + beta 1.2 × premium 6% gives 12.2%.|Equity has zero cost because dividends are optional?|No|Yes|Only when retained earnings fund investment
CAPM relates expected excess return to market beta under its assumptions.|Cost of equity = risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium.|Beta captures…?|Systematic market risk in the model|All business risk|A guaranteed return
Risk-free rates match the cash-flow currency and horizon; equity premiums compensate for market risk.|Rupee and dollar valuations require internally consistent rates and cash flows.|Can one country's bond yield be used blindly for every currency?|No|Yes|Only for global firms
Beta estimates sensitivity to market returns; regression estimates depend on sample and index.|Beta 1.5 implies model exposure greater than market beta 1.|Does beta 1.5 mean the stock always moves 1.5 times the market?|No|Yes|Only on trading days
Unlevering removes financing effects; relevering adds target leverage under specified assumptions.|With zero debt beta, βL = βU[1 + (1 − tax)D/E].|Higher leverage generally raises equity beta under these assumptions?|Yes|No|Only if shares split
Bottom-up beta aggregates comparable business-risk estimates then applies suitable leverage.|Use several comparable unlevered betas rather than one noisy company regression.|Its main benefit is…?|Business-based estimation with less single-company noise|Guaranteed accuracy|No need for peer selection
Country risk and currency consistency require matching operating exposure, cash-flow basis, and discount rates.|A firm earns across countries; incorporation alone does not determine every exposure.|Country risk depends only on registered office?|No|Yes|Only for exporters
Cost of debt is the marginal borrowing return appropriate to credit risk and terms.|Risk-free 5% + spread 3% gives 8% before tax.|Historical coupon necessarily equals current borrowing cost?|No|Yes|Only for fixed-rate debt
Preferred and hybrid capital mix contractual and equity features; classify claims and costs consistently.|A perpetual preferred claim paying ₹8 on price ₹100 has simplified yield 8%.|Its priority and payment terms should be…?|Analysed separately|Assumed identical to common equity|Ignored in WACC
WACC weights required capital costs and appropriate debt tax effects.|60% equity at 12% + 40% debt at 8% after a 25% assumed tax shield gives 9.6%.|FCFF is normally paired with…?|WACC|Only cost of equity|Historical dividend yield
Market weights reflect current claim values; book weights reflect accounting history.|Market equity ₹600 and debt ₹400 give 60% equity weight.|Book and market equity always coincide?|No|Yes|Only for listed firms
Marginal costs price new financing today; historical costs record past funding terms.|Old debt coupon 5% but comparable new borrowing costs 9%.|A new project's financing benchmark should generally reflect…?|Marginal cost|Old coupon alone|Original issue price alone
Modigliani–Miller isolates capital structure under restrictive assumptions; taxes and frictions alter results.|Without taxes or frictions, reshuffling debt and equity does not itself change asset cash flows.|The model proves debt never matters in practice?|No|Yes|Only for profitable firms
Interest tax shields arise where qualifying interest reduces taxable income and the deduction is usable.|₹10 deductible interest at assumed 25% tax saves ₹2.5, if fully usable.|An unusable deduction creates immediate cash savings?|No|Yes|Only when interest is paid
Trade-off theory balances debt benefits and distress costs; pecking order prioritises internal funds, then debt, then equity under information effects.|A firm may prefer retained cash to issuing undervalued shares.|These theories are…?|Explanatory models with conditions|Universal legal rules|Guaranteed financing rankings
Optimal leverage balances tax and funding benefits with distress, agency, and flexibility costs.|Extra borrowing lowers near-term funding cost but raises default exposure.|Maximum available debt is necessarily optimal?|No|Yes|Only if tax-deductible
APV separates unlevered business value from financing effects.|Unlevered value ₹100 + tax-shield PV ₹8 − financing costs ₹3 = ₹105.|APV here is…?|₹105|₹111|₹100
Changing leverage changes equity risk; project rates should match project risk rather than the company average blindly.|A utility enters speculative biotechnology.|Should the utility's historical WACC be applied unchanged?|No|Yes|Only if internally funded
